{"maxId":263,"entries":{"chapter-40-study-applied-clinical-geroscience|Chapter 40 Study: Applied Clinical Geroscience":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Shared biological aging mechanisms may drive multiple chronic diseases and geriatric syndromes; targeting them could extend healthspan (p593-595).":["u3"],"copy|Hallmarks interact: altered metabolism, macromolecular damage, epigenetics, inflammation, stress responses, proteostasis, stem-cell changes and senescence (p594).":["u4"],"copy|Geriatric syndromes share functional risk factors and multifactorial causes. No single molecule explains aging (p595-597).":["u5"],"copy|An upstream target may influence several downstream processes; pathway plausibility alone does not prove clinical benefit (p597, p602-603).":["u6"],"copy|Rapamycin, metformin and senolytics are investigational geroscience approaches; much efficacy evidence remains preclinical (p597-599).":["u7"],"copy|Dasatinib plus quercetin has early human pilot evidence in specific conditions; this does not establish routine treatment for aging (p599).":["u8"],"copy|CALERIE studied 25% calorie restriction in nonobese adults, reporting improvements in disease-risk measures and aging biomarkers (p599).":["u9"],"copy|Healthspan trials target gradual accumulation of disease and disability; resilience trials target recovery after stressors such as surgery or myocardial infarction (p599-600).":["u10"],"copy|Measure clinically meaningful function, independence, disease accumulation and survival; biomarkers may be secondary outcomes (p600).":["u11"],"copy|Include heterogeneous older adults, chronic conditions and care settings. Limited life expectancy and severe impairment require careful consideration of the chance to benefit within the study period (p600-601).":["u12"],"copy|No single clinical-trial endpoint or aging biomarker is universally accepted; a marker should relate to outcomes and respond meaningfully to intervention (p601-602).":["u13"],"copy|Multisystem indices and biomarker panels may be more informative than a single pathway; telomere measures have substantial variability (p602).":["u14"],"copy|DNA-methylation clocks estimate aging-related patterns; evidence that interventions change them remains an evolving research area (p602).":["u15"],"copy|Unregulated clinics are not controlled trials. Most investigational therapies fail, and older adults may be especially vulnerable to harms (p602-603).":["u16"],"copy|Some age-related changes may be adaptive or consequences of aging rather than causes. Clinical geroscience needs combined biological, geriatric and trial expertise (p603).":["u17"],"read-the-endpoint-before-interpreting-the-intervention|Read the endpoint before interpreting the intervention":["u257"],"copy|In mice, low-dose rapamycin can extend both survival and the duration of health. Human studies of a rapamycin analogue in community-dwelling older people examined vaccine responses and reported infections. Improved immune measurements are evidence about that outcome; they do not by themselves show that life is longer (p597–599).":["u258"],"copy|The open-label dasatinib–quercetin pilot involved people with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. Tolerability in a small group with a particular disease is a different question from efficacy for otherwise healthy older people. The chapter presents this work as an early translational step (p599).":["u259"],"copy|After an acute stress such as surgery, measure the patient's return toward baseline function. A trial aimed at delaying multimorbidity would instead follow the accumulation of chronic conditions or disability over time. Decide which question the trial addresses before interpreting a short-term laboratory change (p599–600).":["u260"],"copy|In a post-hoc analysis of CALERIE, a composite index estimated that physiologic age advanced less over 2 years in participants assigned to dietary restriction. This was a model-based result. Different combinations of clinical measurements can estimate biological age, and a change on one index is distinct from an observed gain in years lived independently (p602).":["u261"],"what-evidence-would-support-the-geroscience-hypothesis|What evidence would support the geroscience hypothesis?":["u262"],"distinguishing-the-hallmarks|Distinguishing the hallmarks":["u263"],"why-a-geriatric-syndrome-is-a-demanding-test|Why a geriatric syndrome is a demanding test":["u25"],"how-to-read-a-preclinical-intervention-table|How to read a preclinical intervention table":["u26"],"senotherapeutics-as-a-worked-example-of-translation|Senotherapeutics as a worked example of translation":["u27"],"three-clinical-questions-that-require-different-trial-designs|Three clinical questions that require different trial designs":["u28"],"which-people-should-participate-and-what-constitutes-benefit|Which people should participate, and what constitutes benefit?":["u29"],"distinguish-a-predictor-from-a-validated-treatment-surrogate|Distinguish a predictor from a validated treatment surrogate":["u30"],"counseling-about-a-marketed-intervention|Counseling about a marketed intervention":["u31"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u32"],"copy|Mouse survival, human vaccine response and human survival are separate endpoints (p597–600).":["u33"],"copy|A marker correlated with chronological age may represent adaptation, injury or disease rather than a cause of aging (p601–603).":["u34"],"copy|An open-label disease pilot offers limited evidence when deciding whether an intervention benefits the general older population (p599).":["u35"],"recall-drill|Recall drill":["u36"],"past-exam-questions|Past exam questions":["u37"],"copy|CHAPTER 40 / HAZZARD":["u19"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 40; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u20"],"copy|The geroscience hypothesis":["u21"],"copy|Evidence and trial design":["u22"],"copy|Biomarkers and counseling":["u23"],"copy|Aging is the strongest shared risk factor for many chronic diseases, yet different people of the same chronological age have very different combinations of disease and disability.":["u43"],"copy|Geroscience asks whether some of that shared vulnerability arises from biological processes that participate in several conditions and can be modified.":["u44"],"copy|Figure 40-2 illustrates this relationship with age on the horizontal axis and deaths per 100,000 people per year on the vertical axis, despite the caption calling age the vertical axis (p595).":["u45"],"copy|The claim has testable consequences.":["u46"],"copy|Changing a proposed mechanism should do more than make a measurement look younger: it should alter risk, function, recovery or another outcome that matters to patients.":["u47"],"copy|A drug that treats only one disease effectively may still be valuable without establishing the broader hypothesis (p593–595).":["u48"],"copy|Table 40-1 separates lifespan, ordinarily expressed as a duration of survival, from healthspan, the period of healthy active life before marked functional limitation or dependence.":["u49"],"copy|An intervention could postpone disability without changing the date of death.":["u50"],"copy|It could also extend survival without improving the period lived independently.":["u51"],"copy|A greater average age at death, a longer median lifespan, fewer new diagnoses, more years walking independently and faster recovery from an illness are related but distinct outcomes.":["u52"],"copy|Ask which of them a study measured before deciding what it has established (p593–595, p599–601).":["u53"],"copy|Table 40-2 summarizes the rationale.":["u54"],"copy|Several interacting biological processes change with age; experimental interventions in animals can alter some of those processes and in some settings extend survival or function.":["u55"],"copy|Many chronic diseases cluster as people age, and geriatric syndromes involve shared vulnerabilities.":["u56"],"copy|None of those observations by itself proves that a given age-associated change causes disease in older humans or that modifying it will help.":["u57"],"copy|A useful human experiment needs a credible biological rationale, a population in whom benefit could reasonably occur, tolerability and a clinical endpoint capable of distinguishing benefit from a laboratory change alone (p594–596, p599–602).":["u58"],"copy|Macromolecular damage refers to accumulated injury to proteins, lipids and DNA.":["u59"],"copy|Proteostasis concerns the ability to form, maintain and renew proteins and remove those that are damaged or misfolded.":["u60"],"copy|These concepts overlap but are not synonyms: an intervention might reduce damage, improve the handling of damaged material or alter the cellular response to it.":["u61"],"copy|Altered metabolism includes nutrient sensing, energy balance and signals regulating growth.":["u62"],"copy|Epigenetic change concerns the regulation of gene expression, including methylation and chromatin organization, rather than a simple change in the DNA sequence.":["u63"],"copy|A measurement in any one category may also reflect activity in several others (p594).":["u64"],"copy|Inflammatory signals can arise within one tissue but affect the function of distant organs.":["u65"],"copy|They can both follow existing disease and contribute to its progression.":["u66"],"copy|Stress adaptation spans responses to short-lived challenges and the failure of compensation during persistent stress.":["u67"],"copy|Senescence describes a state that includes cell-cycle arrest and can include a proinflammatory secretory phenotype.":["u68"],"copy|Declining stem-cell or regenerative capacity concerns the ability to renew or repair tissue.":["u69"],"copy|A fall or hospitalization is a clinical stressor, not itself a molecular hallmark.":["u70"],"copy|It can reveal the consequences when these biological changes and pre-existing disease have already narrowed reserve (p594–596, p599–600).":["u71"],"copy|Figure 40-1 places the hallmarks in an interacting framework.":["u72"],"copy|With age, the same tissue can exhibit altered metabolism, accumulated damage, reduced repair and inflammation.":["u73"],"copy|A marker associated with several diseases may be a signal of shared injury rather than a tractable common cause.":["u74"],"copy|Conversely, altering one upstream process could affect several downstream changes.":["u75"],"copy|The distinction is why a mechanistic study may appropriately examine a panel while still requiring a prespecified functional or disease outcome.":["u76"],"copy|A list of hallmarks names plausible questions; it does not rank therapies or establish efficacy (p594–598, p601–603).":["u77"],"copy|A one-to-one model from a single molecular abnormality to a single disease can work for some inherited conditions.":["u78"],"copy|The chapter contrasts that model with the many-to-many relationships involved in falls, delirium, incontinence and frailty.":["u79"],"copy|Several modest risk factors can combine to produce one presentation, while the same vulnerability can contribute to several different syndromes.":["u80"],"copy|A person with reduced mobility, cognitive impairment and limited physiological reserve may fall during an acute illness and may also be vulnerable to delirium or loss of continence.":["u81"],"copy|Identifying one immediate precipitant does not explain why recovery and downstream consequences differ between people (p595–597).":["u82"],"copy|Figures 40-3 and 40-4 extend the familiar clinical framework backward toward biological aging.":["u83"],"copy|Clinical risks such as reduced function act between the underlying biology and the event.":["u84"],"copy|Treatment aimed at a precipitating cause can still be essential even if an upstream mechanism eventually becomes treatable.":["u85"],"copy|A patient with delirium needs assessment for acute illness; the possibility of shared aging biology is not an alternative bedside diagnosis.":["u86"],"copy|The research question is whether altering common vulnerability could reduce the incidence or severity of more than one syndrome or improve resilience to several stresses (p595–597).":["u87"],"copy|Exercise illustrates the distinction between a multicomponent outcome and proof of a unique mechanism.":["u88"],"copy|Improvements in mobility, physical capacity or recovery can matter regardless of whether researchers can attribute them to one pathway.":["u89"],"copy|Several biological and behavioral mechanisms may change together.":["u90"],"copy|A drug with a plausible single target requires separate evidence that the target is reached and that the expected outcome improves.":["u91"],"copy|Similar-looking functional improvements from two interventions do not show that the same molecular change caused them, and failure to affect a single disease marker does not settle the effects on function or independence (p596–598).":["u92"],"copy|Table 40-3 separates reported extension of healthspan and lifespan from evidence that addresses only one of the two.":["u93"],"copy|Its examples with preclinical evidence for both include caloric restriction, exercise, rapamycin, selected renin–angiotensin system drugs, metformin and certain senolytic compounds.":["u94"],"copy|The healthspan group includes some flavonoids, sirtuin activators and inhibitors of the senescence-associated secretory phenotype.":["u95"],"copy|Other interventions have evidence primarily about survival; some effects are specifically described for median survival.":["u96"],"copy|Placement in an experimental category does not mean that a human dose, favorable safety profile or general anti-aging indication is known (p597–598).":["u97"],"copy|Read the footnotes before interpreting a blank outcome.":["u98"],"copy|In some studies lifespan was not assessed, and in others healthspan was unavailable.":["u99"],"copy|Missing evidence is different from evidence that no change occurred.":["u100"],"copy|A treatment tested in one sex, strain, age or stage of life may also yield different results in another group.":["u101"],"copy|If an intervention is started early in life, its effect on long-term development and disease accumulation need not predict the result of starting it after disability is established.":["u102"],"copy|The need to distinguish animal conditions from the clinical circumstances of older adults is one reason the chapter devotes a separate section to translation (p597–600).":["u103"],"copy|The same agent can appear under more than one mechanistic heading.":["u104"],"copy|Rapamycin affects nutrient-signaling pathways and can influence inflammatory or secretory responses, while metformin has established clinical use for diabetes and is discussed as a candidate affecting several aging-related processes.":["u105"],"copy|Evidence supporting an appropriate established indication does not validate its use to prevent aging in otherwise healthy people.":["u106"],"copy|A potential candidate can also carry adverse effects that matter disproportionately in frail patients or when treatment would be taken for years.":["u107"],"copy|No patient dosing regimen for extending human lifespan follows from the table (p597–599, p602–603).":["u108"],"copy|Distinguish the target, the strategy and the claim.":["u109"],"copy|Senescent cells can persist and influence nearby or distant tissues through secreted signals.":["u110"],"copy|A senolytic aims to reduce the burden of such cells, while another class may suppress components of their secretory phenotype.":["u111"],"copy|Evidence of a change in a tissue marker after exposure supports the possibility that the biological target was affected.":["u112"],"copy|It leaves open whether the change is durable, whether another biological function is impaired and whether the person experiences a benefit.":["u113"],"copy|Some changes associated with aging may also be adaptive, so reversing a marker cannot automatically be equated with improving health (p597–599, p601–603).":["u114"],"copy|The chapter describes an early open-label study of intermittent dasatinib plus quercetin in people with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis.":["u115"],"copy|The study asked whether a candidate approach could be given in a specific disease context and provided preliminary human information.":["u116"],"copy|Open-label tolerability in selected participants cannot establish comparative efficacy for mobility, dementia, lifespan or general wellness.":["u117"],"copy|Different questions would require a comparator, adequate follow-up, an outcome appropriate to the target population and systematic assessment of adverse effects.":["u118"],"copy|The chapter's mention of fisetin and other candidates describes a research direction at publication, rather than completed proof for prescribing them (p598–599).":["u119"],"copy|Another early human example is a rapamycin analogue studied in community-dwelling older adults.":["u120"],"copy|Vaccine response and reported infections provided clinically relevant tests of aspects of immune function.":["u121"],"copy|They do not by themselves measure a reduction in multimorbidity or longer survival.":["u122"],"copy|CALERIE studied dietary restriction in nonobese adults, again a different population and intervention.":["u123"],"copy|A signal in a metabolic or immune measure can justify a larger trial when the mechanism and safety make sense.":["u124"],"copy|It cannot be transferred without further evidence to a frail older adult with involuntary weight loss or to a patient using a commercial product of different composition (p598–599, p602).":["u125"],"copy|Figure 40-5 groups clinical tests of aging-directed interventions by the outcome they aim to change: geriatric syndromes, multimorbidity or resilience after a stressor (p598).":["u126"],"copy|One question is whether an intervention delays accumulation of chronic disease.":["u127"],"copy|Figure 40-6 illustrates a hypothetical shift toward later morbidity at a given incidence, rather than a measured treatment effect (p599).":["u128"],"copy|Here meaningful outcomes include time to a newly diagnosed age-related condition, time to a second or third disease, or survival without disease or disability.":["u129"],"copy|Follow-up must be long enough for these events to occur.":["u130"],"copy|If an intervention is already effective against one component of a composite, inspect results for the individual components.":["u131"],"copy|A favorable composite driven entirely by diabetes, for example, would not establish comparable effects on mobility, cancer or cognitive decline.":["u132"],"copy|The chapter describes TAME as a trial design intended to address the translational and regulatory problem, not as completed efficacy evidence (p599–601).":["u133"],"copy|A second question asks whether an intervention prevents or improves a geriatric syndrome.":["u134"],"copy|Choose direct measures of the relevant domain, such as mobility, gait speed, cognition, frailty or ability to perform daily activities.":["u135"],"copy|A patient may notice improved endurance without crossing a threshold for a new diagnosis.":["u136"],"copy|If the person already has severe disability, the endpoint might need to capture meaningful improvement or preservation rather than first onset of impairment.":["u137"],"copy|Conversely, a very healthy participant may have little room for measured improvement during short follow-up.":["u138"],"copy|Match the measurement to baseline function and to the aspect of healthspan that the intervention is expected to change (p599–601).":["u139"],"copy|A third question concerns resilience after a defined stress.":["u140"],"copy|The same operation or acute illness can produce a modest temporary decline in one person and prolonged dependence in another.":["u141"],"copy|Assess function before the stress where possible, the extent of the decline, the time to recover and how close the person comes to their prior baseline.":["u142"],"copy|A single postoperative observation cannot distinguish a smaller initial decline from a faster recovery.":["u143"],"copy|Mortality alone misses an important difference between survivors returning home independently and survivors who require ongoing assistance.":["u144"],"copy|Figure 40-7 shows why serial assessment is informative even when the groups start at similar levels (p599–601).":["u145"],"copy|Including older adults is necessary but insufficient if a study systematically excludes the very patients seen in geriatric practice.":["u146"],"copy|Sex, race and ethnicity, socioeconomic conditions, multimorbidity, function and residence affect generalizability.":["u147"],"copy|Stratification by functional status or a validated risk measure may help disclose a benefit that differs across groups.":["u148"],"copy|Selecting people at appreciable baseline risk can make events observable in a feasible time.":["u149"],"copy|Excessive selection for illness can instead expose people with limited time to benefit to harm.":["u150"],"copy|Record why a particular group was recruited and whether the results reasonably apply to a community, hospital or nursing-facility patient (p600–601).":["u151"],"copy|Table 40-4 includes mortality, disability-free survival, incident diseases, cognitive or physical impairment, syndromes and outcomes related to care and recovery.":["u152"],"copy|These measures need not all move together.":["u153"],"copy|Preventing a new diagnostic code while increasing falls or medication burden may not improve healthspan.":["u154"],"copy|A change in an investigator-selected composite may be hard for a patient to interpret unless the individual outcomes are presented.":["u155"],"copy|Standardized outcomes aid comparison across studies, but the practical meaning of a gain in walking ability can differ between an independently living person and a resident already requiring help with transfers (p600–601).":["u156"],"copy|Table 40-5 starts with a biomarker's association with chronological age and long-term mortality.":["u157"],"copy|It next asks whether it captures several aging-related outcomes, can change over time, and whether change predicts a change in outcome risk.":["u158"],"copy|The most demanding criterion is evidence that it mediates the effect of an intervention already shown to improve clinical outcomes.":["u159"],"copy|A marker may predict who is at high risk without being suitable for deciding whether a treatment works.":["u160"],"copy|A fall in an inflammatory marker after treatment could demonstrate a biological effect while leaving the rate of disability or disease unchanged (p601–602).":["u161"],"copy|A panel can combine signals from distinct systems and may be more informative than a single marker.":["u162"],"copy|In the Cardiovascular Health Study example, a composite of five circulating biomarkers had a mortality hazard ratio of 1.30 per point with a 95% confidence interval of 1.25–1.34, and attenuated the age–mortality association by 25%.":["u163"],"copy|These are prognostic relationships within a particular model.":["u164"],"copy|They do not mean that reducing the score by one point with a drug will lower mortality by the same proportion.":["u165"],"copy|To evaluate that claim would require evidence about changes in the score during an intervention and the resulting clinical outcomes (p602).":["u166"],"copy|Telomere measurements have substantial variability, limiting their individual clinical application.":["u167"],"copy|DNA methylation clocks are calibrated against different targets, from chronological age to risk-related phenotypes.":["u168"],"copy|Two clocks can therefore respond differently to the same intervention without either being a direct measure of years gained.":["u169"],"copy|In the CALERIE post-hoc analysis, a composite physiological estimate advanced less over 2 years in participants assigned to restriction.":["u170"],"copy|Distinguish a modeled change from directly observing longer survival without disability.":["u171"],"copy|Review how a score was derived, whether it was validated outside its development sample, and which as-yet-unmeasured clinical effect is being inferred (p602).":["u172"],"copy|It helps to place three study results side by side.":["u173"],"copy|A measurement of a molecular target before and after dosing can establish target engagement.":["u174"],"copy|A baseline biomarker associated with future disease can help select a higher-risk population for a subsequent trial.":["u175"],"copy|A randomized trial that demonstrates longer disability-free survival answers a clinical question, provided harms are also measured.":["u176"],"copy|None of these designs substitutes for the others.":["u177"],"copy|Combining them can strengthen interpretation if the target, marker and outcome are linked in the same population and time frame; combining unrelated associations across different populations does not demonstrate mediation (p599–602).":["u178"],"copy|Chronological age itself is a good reminder of the distinction.":["u179"],"copy|It predicts many adverse events, but it cannot change in response to treatment.":["u180"],"copy|A clinical trial needs an outcome or measure that can respond and that has a defined direction of benefit.":["u181"],"copy|For a patient, remaining able to shop or dress can be meaningful even when the precise biological mechanism is uncertain.":["u182"],"copy|For validating a biomarker, that uncertainty still matters and should be described separately (p600–602).":["u183"],"copy|Begin with what the patient wants: less fatigue, preservation of independence, fewer illnesses or longer life.":["u184"],"copy|Then identify what the product has actually been shown to do in humans resembling that patient.":["u185"],"copy|Being sold without a prescription, being repurposed from another disease, or having an effect in an animal experiment does not settle dose, interactions, purity, duration or net benefit for an older adult.":["u186"],"copy|A qualifying clinical trial provides defined eligibility, monitoring and comparison; a commercial clinic offering an age estimate or a treatment package does not by itself supply that evidence.":["u187"],"copy|Review current medicines, costs and whether the proposed product would displace established care (p602–603).":["u188"],"copy|The major barriers are practical as well as biological.":["u189"],"copy|Clinical studies need an accepted outcome, enough events or an appropriate stressor, a way to measure biological effects, and investigators familiar with typical older patients.":["u190"],"copy|A treatment acting on an adaptive response could be harmful, or a seemingly favorable mechanism might matter only in a narrow disease state.":["u191"],"copy|For the reader, the most productive interpretation is specific: which mechanism was affected, in whom, for how long, with which clinical result and with what safety information?":["u192"],"copy|Those details determine what can be said to a patient today and which question remains for a future trial (p599–603).":["u193"],"copy|1.":["u194"],"copy|What is the central geroscience hypothesis?":["u195"],"copy|2.":["u196"],"copy|How do healthspan and lifespan differ as endpoints?":["u197"],"copy|3.":["u198"],"copy|Why might one intervention influence several geriatric syndromes?":["u199"],"copy|4.":["u200"],"copy|Which processes are included among the interacting hallmarks?":["u201"],"copy|5.":["u202"],"copy|What did the mouse rapamycin studies establish in their model?":["u203"],"copy|6.":["u204"],"copy|What did early human studies of a rapamycin analogue measure?":["u205"],"copy|7.":["u206"],"copy|In which population was the early dasatinib–quercetin pilot conducted?":["u207"],"copy|8.":["u208"],"copy|What was the dietary intervention studied in CALERIE?":["u209"],"copy|9.":["u210"],"copy|Which trial design suits recovery after myocardial infarction or surgery?":["u211"],"copy|10.":["u212"],"copy|Which design suits a goal of postponing several chronic conditions?":["u213"],"copy|11.":["u214"],"copy|Why pay attention to life expectancy and baseline impairment at enrollment?":["u215"],"copy|12.":["u216"],"copy|What is a useful role for biomarkers in a geroscience trial?":["u217"],"copy|13.":["u218"],"copy|Why is leukocyte telomere length limited as a clinical measure of aging?":["u219"],"copy|14.":["u220"],"copy|What does a methylation clock estimate?":["u221"],"copy|15.":["u222"],"copy|Why might modifying an age-associated pathway have unexpected effects?":["u223"],"copy|No past exam questions are mapped to this chapter in the 2020–2026 reference list.":["u224"],"copy|Answer":["u225","u227","u229","u231","u233","u235","u237","u239","u243","u245","u247","u249","u251","u253","u255"],"copy|Shared mechanisms of biological aging contribute to susceptibility to multiple diseases; modifying them might extend healthy life (p593–595).":["u226"],"copy|Healthspan concerns the duration of health and function. Lifespan measures survival and can include years lived with disability (p594, p599–600).":["u228"],"copy|The syndromes share upstream biological and functional risk factors, although each also has other causes (p595–597).":["u230"],"copy|Examples are altered metabolism, molecular damage, disrupted protein maintenance, inflammation, stem-cell changes and senescence (p594).":["u232"],"copy|Rapamycin inhibition of mTOR could extend both survival and healthspan in the experiments discussed (p597–598).":["u234"],"copy|Immune responses, including vaccine response, and reported infections in community-dwelling older adults (p599).":["u236"],"copy|People with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis in an open-label pilot. Its results do not establish a general anti-aging treatment (p599).":["u238"],"copy|It targeted":["u240"],"copy|25% calorie restriction":["u241"],"copy|in nonobese adults and evaluated risk measures and aging biomarkers (p599).":["u242"],"copy|A resilience trial, which examines recovery from a defined acute stressor (p599–600).":["u244"],"copy|A healthspan trial tracking gradual accumulation of disease or disability and meaningful functional outcomes (p599–600).":["u246"],"copy|They affect the opportunity to experience benefit within follow-up and the relevance of endpoints to that participant (p600–601).":["u248"],"copy|They can test biological responses alongside clinical outcomes; their relation to meaningful benefit requires validation (p600–602).":["u250"],"copy|It shows substantial variability despite associations with age and some age-related conditions (p602).":["u252"],"copy|An aging-related pattern from changes in DNA methylation. Its movement with an intervention requires interpretation against outcomes (p602).":["u254"],"copy|An observed change may be adaptive or downstream of another process, and aging mechanisms interact (p602–603).":["u256"]}}
